From 0009-nouveau-pcie-link-retrain Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Krzysztof Kowalski <krzysztof7kowalski@gmail.com>
Date: Tue, 26 Aug 2026 00:00:00 +0200
Subject: [PATCH 0009/0010] nouveau: retrain PCIe link to max after resume

After a hard power-cycle of the GT 750M (apple-gmux cutting the core /
VRAM / PCIe rails on the MacBookPro11,3) the PCIe link frequently comes
back at the lowest common speed (2.5GT/s) instead of the negotiated
5GT/s, silently regressing the dGPU to gen1 (raport 22 section B.4, raport 48
section 3).  nvkm_pcie_init() already raises the cap/lnkctl registers to the
maximum via the chip's ->pcie.init() (gk104_pcie_init), but nothing ever
triggers a link retrain: nvkm_pcie_set_link() is only called when an
explicit speed was requested (pci->pcie.speed != -1), which is not the
case for a plain runtime resume.

Extend nvkm_pcie_init(): when no explicit speed is pending, read the
current negotiated speed and, if it is below the card maximum, retrain to
the maximum through the existing nvkm_pcie_set_link() path (same HW
sequence the pstate code uses).  The retrain is a no-op whenever the link
is already at the maximum speed, so boot paths and already-fast links are
unaffected.

Signed-off-by: Krzysztof Kowalski <krzysztof7kowalski@gmail.com>
---
 drivers/gpu/drm/nouveau/nvkm/subdev/pci/pcie.c | 21 +++++++++++++++++++++
 1 file changed, 22 insertions(+), 1 deletion(-)

--- a/drivers/gpu/drm/nouveau/nvkm/subdev/pci/pcie.c
+++ b/drivers/gpu/drm/nouveau/nvkm/subdev/pci/pcie.c
@@ -105,8 +105,29 @@
 	if (pci->func->pcie.init)
 		pci->func->pcie.init(pci);
 
-	if (pci->pcie.speed != -1)
+	if (pci->pcie.speed != -1) {
 		nvkm_pcie_set_link(pci, pci->pcie.speed, pci->pcie.width);
+	} else if (pci->func->pcie.cur_speed && pci->func->pcie.max_speed) {
+		enum nvkm_pcie_speed cur_speed = pci->func->pcie.cur_speed(pci);
+		enum nvkm_pcie_speed max_speed = pci->func->pcie.max_speed(pci);
+
+		/*
+		 * After a hard power-cycle (apple-gmux cutting the PCIe power
+		 * rails on the MacBookPro11,3) the dGPU link frequently trains
+		 * back at the lowest common speed (2.5GT/s) even though the
+		 * card supports 5GT/s: ->pcie.init() above already raised the
+		 * cap/lnkctl registers to the maximum, but nothing triggers a
+		 * retrain.  When no explicit speed was requested, retrain the
+		 * link to the maximum the card supports so a resumed GPU does
+		 * not silently regress to PCIe gen1.
+		 */
+		if (cur_speed >= NVKM_PCIE_SPEED_2_5 && cur_speed < max_speed) {
+			nvkm_debug(subdev, "retraining link from %s to %s\n",
+				   nvkm_pcie_speeds[cur_speed],
+				   nvkm_pcie_speeds[max_speed]);
+			nvkm_pcie_set_link(pci, max_speed, pci->pcie.width);
+		}
+	}
 
 	return 0;
 }
